WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:50.058 --> 00:00:54.220
Welcome everyone,

00:00:54.220 --> 00:01:01.817
we'll get started in just a minute.

00:01:08.982 --> 00:01:17.170
All right,
welcome to leadership of the society.

00:01:17.170 --> 00:01:21.080
In this season we're talking
about reimaging work post-COVID.

00:01:21.080 --> 00:01:25.479
And I'm excited to have three guests today
to talk about shifting demographics in

00:01:25.479 --> 00:01:26.870
the workplace.

00:01:26.870 --> 00:01:29.605
So, I our three guests
are Laura Carstensen,

00:01:29.605 --> 00:01:32.632
professor of psychology
at Stanford University.

00:01:32.632 --> 00:01:36.408
Also the founding director of
the Stanford Center on Longevity, and

00:01:36.408 --> 00:01:39.794
the author of A Long Bright Future,
Happiness, Health, and

00:01:39.794 --> 00:01:43.750
Financial Security in an Age
of Increased Longevity.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:45.445
We also have Manu Meel.

00:01:45.445 --> 00:01:47.707
Manu serves the CEO of BridgeUSA,

00:01:47.707 --> 00:01:52.470
a national organization that's
investing in the future of democracy.

00:01:52.470 --> 00:01:56.559
And Jennifer Liu, and Jennifer Liu is
a workplace reporter for CNBC Make It.

00:01:56.559 --> 00:01:58.915
Where she writes about
the culture of work and

00:01:58.915 --> 00:02:02.325
she also contributes to
Make It'd Millennial Money franchise,

00:02:02.325 --> 00:02:06.570
a series that details how people earn,
spend, and save their money.

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:11.464
Thank you all for
agreeing to be with me here today.

00:02:12.561 --> 00:02:13.320
&gt;&gt; Thanks for having us.
&gt;&gt; Great, so,

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:16.758
let me start off by just recommending
something that we've all heard a lot

00:02:16.758 --> 00:02:18.050
about recently.

00:02:18.050 --> 00:02:20.794
So the workforce is aging.

00:02:20.794 --> 00:02:23.377
So according to the US, I mean,

00:02:23.377 --> 00:02:28.743
according to on the National Council
on Compensation Insurance,

00:02:28.743 --> 00:02:32.982
we can talk about why
they're doing these studies.

00:02:32.982 --> 00:02:35.279
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:02:35.279 --> 00:02:37.794
&gt;&gt; The US labor force aged 65 and

00:02:37.794 --> 00:02:43.211
older is expected to grow by more
than 50% in the next ten years,

00:02:43.211 --> 00:02:47.490
making it 10% of the total labor force.

00:02:47.490 --> 00:02:51.808
So the workers aged 65 and older are
predicted to be more than 10% of the total

00:02:51.808 --> 00:02:54.240
labor force in the next ten years.

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.885
What challenges and opportunities do you
see tied to the aging of the workforce?

00:02:58.885 --> 00:03:02.397
And Laura,
I'd love to hear from you first.

00:03:02.397 --> 00:03:07.190
&gt;&gt; Sure, I think we hear a lot
about the aging of the workforce.

00:03:07.190 --> 00:03:10.158
I think the bigger story embedded

00:03:10.158 --> 00:03:14.934
in this is the age
diversification of the workforce.

00:03:14.934 --> 00:03:20.364
So this is the first time in human
history where we have had five and

00:03:20.364 --> 00:03:26.614
six birth cohorts working together in
the same workforce at the same time.

00:03:26.614 --> 00:03:29.088
We've just never had this before.

00:03:29.088 --> 00:03:35.416
So, when we talk about aging workforce, it
kind of focuses us on those 65 and older.

00:03:35.416 --> 00:03:40.485
But really the issue is how do people
from very different backgrounds,

00:03:40.485 --> 00:03:45.986
very different ages, work together in
a way that optimizes that diversity?

00:03:45.986 --> 00:03:49.200
&gt;&gt; Great, and
what other opportunities there?

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.722
I'm curious how you think about that.

00:03:52.962 --> 00:03:56.720
&gt;&gt; The opportunities
are enormous in my view.

00:03:56.720 --> 00:04:03.345
We don't have a lot of research yet
on mixed-age workforces,

00:04:03.345 --> 00:04:11.220
but the studies that are here now suggest
that productivity may well go up.

00:04:12.700 --> 00:04:18.395
So there's a famous study that was done
in an automobile manufacturing plant

00:04:18.395 --> 00:04:24.001
in Europe where they compared productivity
on an assembly line of work teams

00:04:24.001 --> 00:04:29.630
that were all young, work teams that
were old, and mixed-age work teams.

00:04:30.910 --> 00:04:36.434
And what they found is that the mixed-age
work teams were most productive of all.

00:04:36.434 --> 00:04:39.880
The young teams were really fast,
and they made a lot of mistakes.

00:04:40.940 --> 00:04:46.303
And the older teams were slower, but
they essentially didn't make mistakes.

00:04:46.303 --> 00:04:50.020
And the other piece of this from other
studies is that when young people make

00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:53.455
mistakes in manufacturing and
selling, they're very expensive.

00:04:53.455 --> 00:04:57.240
[LAUGH] When older people make mistakes,
they're not as expensive.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:02.346
So to me the opportunity is
you put the ages together and

00:05:02.346 --> 00:05:08.118
you have teams of people where
you have experienced workers,

00:05:08.118 --> 00:05:14.889
who have lots of knowledge about that
field and their particular tasks.

00:05:14.889 --> 00:05:19.801
And you mix them in with young
people who are ambitious and

00:05:19.801 --> 00:05:25.240
fast learners and active, and
you see really great outcomes.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:29.176
So, we've seen this kind of
increase in productivity now,

00:05:29.176 --> 00:05:33.817
not just in assembly line workers,
but in lots of different contexts.

00:05:33.817 --> 00:05:36.240
And I think that's the possibility.

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:39.770
But the trick is what is that mix?

00:05:39.770 --> 00:05:41.340
What's the optimal mix?

00:05:41.340 --> 00:05:42.218
How do you do it?

00:05:42.218 --> 00:05:43.486
How do you put people together?

00:05:43.486 --> 00:05:48.283
And how do you make sure everybody's
got a voice in those teams?

00:05:48.283 --> 00:05:53.449
&gt;&gt; Yes, what I hear a lot about lately
is the tension between older workers and

00:05:53.449 --> 00:05:55.310
younger workers.

00:05:55.310 --> 00:06:01.061
And I'm curious, Jennifer, you write
in this space of the culture of work.

00:06:01.061 --> 00:06:08.066
What do you see in terms of the tensions
between younger and older workers?

00:06:08.066 --> 00:06:08.940
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's a great question.

00:06:08.940 --> 00:06:15.365
Earlier this year, my team and
I at CNBC Make It did a big

00:06:15.365 --> 00:06:20.550
series around middle-aged millennials.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:23.297
When you think of millennials
in the workplace,

00:06:23.297 --> 00:06:26.852
you might still think of young
professionals, recent grads.

00:06:26.852 --> 00:06:31.770
When in fact some of the oldest
millennials are turning 40 this year.

00:06:31.770 --> 00:06:37.352
And when you think of reaching 40,
these workers are reaching or

00:06:37.352 --> 00:06:41.738
still working towards some
major life milestones,

00:06:41.738 --> 00:06:47.033
starting a family, buying a home,
paying off student loans.

00:06:47.033 --> 00:06:52.511
And all of that translates to
what they bring to the workplace.

00:06:52.511 --> 00:06:57.972
The stereotype that millennials are young,
and hungry,

00:06:57.972 --> 00:07:03.105
and ready to job hop to find
the next best opportunity.

00:07:03.105 --> 00:07:06.932
While that is true of all younger workers,

00:07:06.932 --> 00:07:13.205
people who are in this kind of
middle-age reaching middle management

00:07:13.205 --> 00:07:18.325
track are feeling these intense,
personal pressures.

00:07:18.325 --> 00:07:25.370
And that impacts the decisions that they
make in their careers as far as going for

00:07:25.370 --> 00:07:29.904
promotions, finding
opportunities to advance.

00:07:29.904 --> 00:07:35.192
There has been a decades long
disinvestment in learning and

00:07:35.192 --> 00:07:39.326
development programs in corporate America.

00:07:39.326 --> 00:07:44.166
So the moving from employer to employer,

00:07:44.166 --> 00:07:48.044
of course, leads to attrition.

00:07:48.044 --> 00:07:53.290
And establishing that
company loyalty is not maybe

00:07:53.290 --> 00:07:59.470
necessarily at the top of
these professionals' minds.

00:07:59.470 --> 00:08:05.233
And of course, that can breed some
tension between people who are Younger,

00:08:05.233 --> 00:08:09.299
working toward establishing
themselves in their career have

00:08:09.299 --> 00:08:13.530
put in at this point a decade plus
of their lives into their work.

00:08:13.530 --> 00:08:17.614
But are still having trouble
reaching levels of management or

00:08:17.614 --> 00:08:21.855
leadership, where they see
themselves making decisions, and

00:08:21.855 --> 00:08:25.170
steering the future of a company,
or operation.

00:08:25.170 --> 00:08:31.330
And maybe not necessarily seeing that
support from senior leaders as well.

00:08:31.330 --> 00:08:34.320
&gt;&gt; So that leads me to the question,
what do you think of is the,

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:38.540
where's the power dynamic right now
between older and younger workers?

00:08:38.540 --> 00:08:42.832
It's interesting because on the one hand,
there's certainly ageism in the country,

00:08:42.832 --> 00:08:44.805
there's no doubt that that is an issue.

00:08:44.805 --> 00:08:49.551
On the other hand, I hear a lot
about younger workers failing,

00:08:49.551 --> 00:08:55.120
not having the opportunity to ascend
to decision making positions.

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:57.527
So how do you all see the tension, or

00:08:57.527 --> 00:09:02.740
the power dynamic playing out right
now between older and younger workers?

00:09:02.740 --> 00:09:04.339
Manu, I'd love to hear your thoughts.

00:09:05.420 --> 00:09:07.997
&gt;&gt; Sure, and thank you all so
much for having me and

00:09:07.997 --> 00:09:10.909
great to be in conversation
with Jennifer and Laura.

00:09:10.909 --> 00:09:13.809
And I do have to certainly
relate to the line that Laura

00:09:13.809 --> 00:09:17.878
said at the end which is that younger
teams are a lot more prone to mistakes.

00:09:17.878 --> 00:09:20.717
As someone that is now full
time in a dorm room project,

00:09:20.717 --> 00:09:23.929
I can tell you that our mistakes
far outnumber our successes,

00:09:23.929 --> 00:09:27.030
and right now we're in a hiring pinch for
older workers.

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:30.450
So, that's sort of the perspective
that I'm bringing.

00:09:30.450 --> 00:09:34.381
The other perspective I'm bringing is
that, it was my first full time year of

00:09:34.381 --> 00:09:37.342
work and I've only known
the pandemic as a workspace, and

00:09:37.342 --> 00:09:39.775
that's actually a very
interesting dynamic.

00:09:39.775 --> 00:09:43.897
For when you look at a lot of folks that
are especially at the older end of gen Z,

00:09:43.897 --> 00:09:46.880
I don't even know technically
if I qualify as gen Z, but

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:49.615
I don't also qualify as a millennial.

00:09:49.615 --> 00:09:51.480
So somewhere in the middle there, but

00:09:51.480 --> 00:09:54.627
basically you have this string of
workers from the age of about,

00:09:54.627 --> 00:09:58.480
I would say 21 to 23, that have never
actually been to the workplace.

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:02.738
And so, that is an interesting dynamic
that a lot of people have to contend with

00:10:02.738 --> 00:10:04.752
when they do start opening offices.

00:10:04.752 --> 00:10:09.356
To your direct question about what
are sort of the power dynamics and

00:10:09.356 --> 00:10:10.824
the opportunities?

00:10:10.824 --> 00:10:15.485
I think the opportunity is that you
have a tremendous opportunity for

00:10:15.485 --> 00:10:16.780
social cohesion.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:20.531
I mean when you see five different
age cohorts in one place,

00:10:20.531 --> 00:10:25.181
I mean right now I'm hard pressed to
find another area of social space, or

00:10:25.181 --> 00:10:30.150
impact where you have that much diversity
of views, viewpoints, and ages.

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:33.693
That means that your workplace is
essentially what Tocqueville used to

00:10:33.693 --> 00:10:37.178
call is your voluntary political
association, because I don't know what

00:10:37.178 --> 00:10:40.460
else is town square these days,
and so that's what you have.

00:10:40.460 --> 00:10:44.629
That's a huge opportunity point, not just
from a political sense, but from a from

00:10:44.629 --> 00:10:49.180
a social and melting pot sense, where
we're seeking more and more connection.

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:53.748
But to your point about I would say
power dynamics, especially now,

00:10:53.748 --> 00:10:59.440
I think young people often underestimate
the power of being young in the workplace.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:03.307
I mean, from my experience what I've
noticed and this might be very relevant to

00:11:03.307 --> 00:11:06.390
me, but from folks that are now
entering the workspace.

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:10.153
There is a lot of opportunity that a lot
of younger people have had because of

00:11:10.153 --> 00:11:12.678
their initiative and
drive that they've shown.

00:11:12.678 --> 00:11:16.554
And a lot of your older power dynamics,
I don't think are as relevant in some of

00:11:16.554 --> 00:11:20.432
your newer industries, especially when
you look at tech, venture capital,

00:11:20.432 --> 00:11:23.160
a lot of the industries a lot
of GSB folks be going into.

00:11:23.160 --> 00:11:25.504
Because they're are a lot
more flexible and

00:11:25.504 --> 00:11:29.446
I think the success is less age dependent,
more experienced dependent.

00:11:29.446 --> 00:11:33.836
Last point that I would just
say in terms of power dynamics.

00:11:33.836 --> 00:11:38.342
The disadvantage here and the risk is that
I think you're at a unique moment in this

00:11:38.342 --> 00:11:42.459
country's history where tying and
separating work, and political, and

00:11:42.459 --> 00:11:45.680
social issues,
becoming increasingly more difficult.

00:11:45.680 --> 00:11:48.610
I'm often in conversations
with a lot of industry leaders

00:11:48.610 --> 00:11:51.478
where they have to decide whether or
not they have to issue

00:11:51.478 --> 00:11:55.819
a statement on let's say the voting rights
bill, or various other cultural issues.

00:11:55.819 --> 00:11:58.294
And I'm not the one that's trying to
bring in politics to the room, but

00:11:58.294 --> 00:11:59.830
that's the decisions that are being made.

00:11:59.830 --> 00:12:04.595
And so the dynamic that introduces is, you
have a generation of value differences at

00:12:04.595 --> 00:12:08.830
the political level that can create
a lot of tension in the workplace.

00:12:08.830 --> 00:12:12.575
And I think that is something that
a lot of leaders are going to have to

00:12:12.575 --> 00:12:13.470
contend with.

00:12:13.470 --> 00:12:17.778
&gt;&gt; I'm happy you brought up the kind of
tensions between the generations,

00:12:17.778 --> 00:12:22.587
because you and I are both referenced
the broad diversity in the workplace, and

00:12:22.587 --> 00:12:25.940
the potential benefits
associated with that.

00:12:25.940 --> 00:12:30.255
But if we've learnt anything
over the last few years is that

00:12:30.255 --> 00:12:34.150
diversity can also lead to
a lot of conflict, right?

00:12:34.150 --> 00:12:38.750
And so, I'm wondering how you are managing
that conflict, and it's come up, right?

00:12:38.750 --> 00:12:42.970
So you hear people talking about
boomers in discouraging ways, right?

00:12:42.970 --> 00:12:47.662
And then by the same token, you hear a lot
of negative stereotypes of millennials and

00:12:47.662 --> 00:12:49.110
Gen Zs, right?

00:12:49.110 --> 00:12:53.047
How do you, when you bring all these
people together, how do you create

00:12:53.047 --> 00:12:57.261
the situation so that you get the benefits
associated with that diversity?

00:12:57.261 --> 00:13:01.486
As opposed to getting balkanization
like these fractures along these

00:13:01.486 --> 00:13:05.712
generational lines, especially
with the differences in values, or

00:13:05.712 --> 00:13:09.020
political opinions, and
tied to those differences.

00:13:11.510 --> 00:13:14.496
So I'll [CROSSTALK] Laura,
you go ahead I'd love it.

00:13:14.496 --> 00:13:18.586
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Because I was thinking of what Monica

00:13:18.586 --> 00:13:24.502
said, and social psychology,
probably the best supported

00:13:24.502 --> 00:13:29.954
way to bring different kinds
of communities together,

00:13:29.954 --> 00:13:37.710
is by creating a group where everybody's
working toward the same goal.

00:13:37.710 --> 00:13:41.776
And I think the workplace is
an excellent place for that to happen,

00:13:41.776 --> 00:13:45.994
because you have the same productivity
goal, the same output goal.

00:13:45.994 --> 00:13:50.925
And bringing people together with
different perspectives when you're

00:13:50.925 --> 00:13:55.540
working towards the same goal is
really a great way to unite people.

00:13:55.540 --> 00:13:58.015
I see the workforce,
whether paid or unpaid,

00:13:58.015 --> 00:14:01.698
volunteer workforce is another way
to bring generations together,

00:14:01.698 --> 00:14:05.264
where you're coming together
because you care about something.

00:14:05.264 --> 00:14:10.609
So, people work in a particular kind of
a discipline because it matters to them,

00:14:10.609 --> 00:14:11.200
right?

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:16.183
So, there are some shared values and
you put them together, and

00:14:16.183 --> 00:14:20.900
that's where I think you start
to see lots of common ground.

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:25.803
&gt;&gt; And if I could just add to that,

00:14:25.803 --> 00:14:30.323
I think when you're thinking about
where diversity can often produce more

00:14:30.323 --> 00:14:35.069
discord than sort of what you were talking
about unity towards a certain goal.

00:14:35.069 --> 00:14:40.883
I think one of the biggest factors we've
seen in our organization for example, and

00:14:40.883 --> 00:14:46.530
we work in different continents both at
the university and high school level, so

00:14:46.530 --> 00:14:52.490
you both have a diversity in Gen Z itself,
but then you have cultural diversity.

00:14:52.490 --> 00:14:54.745
And on top of that you have
layered age diversity, so

00:14:54.745 --> 00:14:57.200
it's a diversity melting pot,
let's just say that.

00:14:57.200 --> 00:15:00.925
And the big challenge there,
we've noticed is miscommunication and

00:15:00.925 --> 00:15:04.680
lack of ability for people to
actually raise concerns and thoughts.

00:15:04.680 --> 00:15:09.058
I think there's often this, especially
now with our cultural moment, workers,

00:15:09.058 --> 00:15:11.761
regardless of what age you are,
what you believe.

00:15:11.761 --> 00:15:16.703
Everyone's thinking about that certain
thing that's happening in the world, and

00:15:16.703 --> 00:15:19.625
no one knows how to address it or
talk about it, and

00:15:19.625 --> 00:15:23.400
now your company's talking about
that issue in a public light.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:27.310
And the best way I think to capture
those diverse viewpoints is to make and

00:15:27.310 --> 00:15:30.389
allow spaces in companies,
spaces in organizations for

00:15:30.389 --> 00:15:32.456
people to have those conversations.

00:15:32.456 --> 00:15:34.069
To have those smooth dialogues,

00:15:34.069 --> 00:15:37.072
to be able to facilitate
intergenerational communication.

00:15:37.072 --> 00:15:41.021
And to not shy away from the issues
that are often seen as obstacles towards

00:15:41.021 --> 00:15:45.670
company productivity, but instead use them
as reasons for people to be comfortable,

00:15:45.670 --> 00:15:48.100
and engaging expressing their views.

00:15:48.100 --> 00:15:51.158
I think when you position your
company as very diverse, but

00:15:51.158 --> 00:15:55.687
don't allow diverse opinions to percolate,
then I think there's a counterintuitive

00:15:55.687 --> 00:15:59.860
effect that can actually hinder
the productivity of diversity.

00:15:59.860 --> 00:16:01.910
I agree, couldn't agree more.

00:16:01.910 --> 00:16:05.077
And that again, when we talk about
sort of what's the big story?

00:16:05.077 --> 00:16:08.664
Is it an ageing workforce or
is it an aged diverse workforce?

00:16:08.664 --> 00:16:12.930
And then we talked about cohort
differences, the differences may really

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:16.506
come about more because of ethnic,
and racial diversity, as

00:16:16.506 --> 00:16:20.867
a function of both cohort in the country,
than it does as a function of age.

00:16:20.867 --> 00:16:25.965
But we tend to meld those things,
to conflate them, and

00:16:25.965 --> 00:16:33.128
so we talk about older people as being
more prejudice or bigoted or something.

00:16:33.128 --> 00:16:38.527
But it really reflects the kind of
population which they were born into and

00:16:38.527 --> 00:16:43.158
grew up with more than it does age,
I guess is what I'm saying.

00:16:43.158 --> 00:16:48.138
So I think we need to be careful about
what we think of as the age problem,

00:16:48.138 --> 00:16:52.703
and what we think of as the diversity
problem, which is real, and

00:16:52.703 --> 00:16:54.707
one that we need to address.

00:16:54.707 --> 00:16:59.547
&gt;&gt; And speaking of that, how do you, so
no matter how open we are to the age

00:16:59.547 --> 00:17:05.260
diversity in the workplace,
there will be generational shifts, right?

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:08.290
How do we manage those effectively?

00:17:08.290 --> 00:17:09.490
What is the expectation?

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:12.757
When should people who
are older make space for

00:17:12.757 --> 00:17:15.610
people that are younger, is that a way?

00:17:15.610 --> 00:17:18.190
How should we even think
about that question?

00:17:18.190 --> 00:17:21.550
And how do you manage generational
shifts and power gracefully?

00:17:24.050 --> 00:17:29.026
&gt;&gt; People tend to think
that of workforces as

00:17:29.026 --> 00:17:33.750
zero-sum games as a fixed pot.

00:17:33.750 --> 00:17:36.870
And that is not a good way
to think of the workforce.

00:17:36.870 --> 00:17:42.671
The more people who work in a population,
the more jobs to get created,

00:17:42.671 --> 00:17:48.101
and so it is a zero-sum game, and
we need to first recognize that.

00:17:48.101 --> 00:17:52.828
Second, there appears to be
lots of just sort of natural,

00:17:52.828 --> 00:17:58.720
pro-social investments that older
workers have for younger workers.

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:02.396
So there's an awful lot
of mentoring that goes

00:18:02.396 --> 00:18:06.083
on around the water
fountain in the hallways.

00:18:06.083 --> 00:18:11.631
With the older workers helping younger
workers advance their own careers,

00:18:11.631 --> 00:18:17.190
and that's the kind of really nice
developmental trend that we seem to see.

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:24.059
UC Berkeley had a program years ago where
they were trying to get rid of the older

00:18:24.059 --> 00:18:24.971
professors.

00:18:24.971 --> 00:18:28.799
And so they were giving people really
sweet buyout deals to go away,

00:18:28.799 --> 00:18:30.822
and of course two things happened.

00:18:30.822 --> 00:18:35.493
One was the top professors just left and
got jobs at other universities and

00:18:35.493 --> 00:18:38.520
took, [LAUGH] the sweetheart
deal too to leave.

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:43.908
But what happened is that the assistant
professors were struggling, and

00:18:43.908 --> 00:18:47.634
because they didn't have older
colleagues who were saying,

00:18:47.634 --> 00:18:52.050
you should talk to so-and-so, or
let me introduce you to so-and-so, or

00:18:52.050 --> 00:18:54.893
there's an opportunity for
publication here.

00:18:54.893 --> 00:18:58.310
And so
I think we need to also be aware of that.

00:18:58.310 --> 00:19:02.689
And that's what tends to happen just
naturally when you put mixed generations

00:19:02.689 --> 00:19:03.970
together.

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:07.795
&gt;&gt; I hear that, and I see some of
that certainly in the academy,

00:19:07.795 --> 00:19:10.951
being a professor,
I have had that experienced.

00:19:10.951 --> 00:19:14.902
And I've also talked to some people who
are, I'll just say let's say younger than

00:19:14.902 --> 00:19:18.580
I am, we should kind of define what we
mean by young, because I think people.

00:19:18.580 --> 00:19:21.419
[LAUGH] Wherever the lines
are drawn between older,

00:19:21.419 --> 00:19:25.446
middle-aged, that young that varies,
and we're talking to, but for

00:19:25.446 --> 00:19:29.757
now I'll just stay with younger people
I talk to, let's call it under 30.

00:19:29.757 --> 00:19:32.724
&gt;&gt; Yeah.
&gt;&gt; And some of them are expressing,

00:19:32.724 --> 00:19:36.500
I would say,
anger toward older generations.

00:19:36.500 --> 00:19:38.703
And here's something that I hear, and

00:19:38.703 --> 00:19:41.450
I'm going to push this to
you Jennifer in a second.

00:19:42.780 --> 00:19:47.850
The older generation basically leveraged
our future to take care of themselves.

00:19:47.850 --> 00:19:50.900
They love this this mess with the climate.

00:19:50.900 --> 00:19:54.151
And now we're at an age
where they could buy homes,

00:19:54.151 --> 00:19:59.310
we can't buy homes right now, we can't
afford to have families right now.

00:19:59.310 --> 00:20:04.517
If all of this is in part
because of the way people ahead

00:20:04.517 --> 00:20:10.980
of us manage the economy for
their own benefit at our expense.

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:14.256
So I'm curious Jennifer and
you because you work with,

00:20:14.256 --> 00:20:17.755
thinking about some millennials and
maybe younger folks.

00:20:17.755 --> 00:20:22.360
Is that something that you hear,
and how do you think about,

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:26.438
how that's playing out in
terms of this age dynamic?

00:20:26.438 --> 00:20:30.659
&gt;&gt; Yeah, certainly, I've absolutely
heard those exact sentiments

00:20:30.659 --> 00:20:35.110
from numerous sources that I have
spoken with over the past few months.

00:20:36.900 --> 00:20:44.740
And there are certainly anecdotal
experiences that they can draw from there.

00:20:44.740 --> 00:20:48.917
What they see in their
own workplace dynamic,

00:20:48.917 --> 00:20:54.820
as well as macro level shifts in
the economy and the labor market.

00:20:54.820 --> 00:21:00.527
I think the key is going back to Laura,
what you brought up,

00:21:00.527 --> 00:21:06.593
there's a huge opportunity for
mentorship in the workplace.

00:21:06.593 --> 00:21:12.013
And I think that could help
bridge some of the divides and

00:21:12.013 --> 00:21:17.088
address some of the tensions
that if younger workers

00:21:17.088 --> 00:21:23.199
don't see that they have a upward
path within their position or

00:21:23.199 --> 00:21:26.454
their role at a certain company.

00:21:26.454 --> 00:21:31.613
But being paired with someone who
has been with the employer for

00:21:31.613 --> 00:21:35.308
a longer period of time,
they can draw from,

00:21:35.308 --> 00:21:38.836
their years of experience to draw them up.

00:21:38.836 --> 00:21:44.076
And I think a big issue especially for
younger workers is not seeing,

00:21:44.076 --> 00:21:47.688
what is my contribution
to this organization,

00:21:47.688 --> 00:21:52.140
am I just following
the footsteps of someone else?

00:21:52.140 --> 00:21:57.042
But I think having that
dialogue in that two-way street

00:21:57.042 --> 00:22:02.540
brainstorming, this is how
things have been done.

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:07.295
But also recognizing and
finding value, and

00:22:07.295 --> 00:22:12.686
bring in new ideas could
ease some of that tension.

00:22:12.686 --> 00:22:17.461
&gt;&gt; And I would just say that when you're
thinking about sort of the animosity

00:22:17.461 --> 00:22:21.390
that younger generations have
towards older generations,

00:22:21.390 --> 00:22:24.393
I'm going to go a little
bit against the grain and

00:22:24.393 --> 00:22:29.267
sort of the consensus here, and
actually disagree a little bit with that.

00:22:29.267 --> 00:22:34.088
And that what I've found is not so
much across the board sort of Gen Z and

00:22:34.088 --> 00:22:35.734
younger millennials.

00:22:35.734 --> 00:22:39.890
I'm suppose you're talking about,
I would say 23,

00:22:39.890 --> 00:22:43.527
to sort of 27 and
then people from 18 to 23.

00:22:43.527 --> 00:22:47.526
So that's sort of the age range that
I'm talking about if we're trying to be

00:22:47.526 --> 00:22:48.962
specific, and it's not so

00:22:48.962 --> 00:22:52.791
much a criticism of older generations
that I've seen across the board.

00:22:52.791 --> 00:22:55.293
That certainly exists to
sort of Jennifer's point,

00:22:55.293 --> 00:22:57.080
you hear that certainly on the coast.

00:22:57.080 --> 00:22:57.722
And you hear that,

00:22:57.722 --> 00:23:00.225
especially with students that
are graduating from elite universities.

00:23:00.225 --> 00:23:02.655
But that's the key difference is,

00:23:02.655 --> 00:23:07.839
the other big strain that I've seen is
actually more of an anti-elitism and

00:23:07.839 --> 00:23:12.680
anti-institutional, ethic and
anti-authority ethic.

00:23:12.680 --> 00:23:16.445
You'll see this in very basic things, like
the number one political podcast right

00:23:16.445 --> 00:23:19.349
now in the country is not from
your establishment news outlets,

00:23:19.349 --> 00:23:21.580
it's actually from
independent journalists.

00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:23.542
The reason why things like Substack,

00:23:23.542 --> 00:23:26.302
Clubhouse, enjoying these
trends are catching on is

00:23:26.302 --> 00:23:30.659
because the younger workforce is actually
a lot more focused on criticizing trust,

00:23:30.659 --> 00:23:34.058
and institutions, and less focused,
I think in policy issues.

00:23:34.058 --> 00:23:36.625
And that the policy issues
might manifest in sort

00:23:36.625 --> 00:23:38.826
of that anti-institutional critique.

00:23:38.826 --> 00:23:41.726
But when I look at it as
anti-institutional as opposed

00:23:41.726 --> 00:23:43.681
to they're critical of climate, or

00:23:43.681 --> 00:23:47.350
they're critical of how the older
generation has handled x, y, z.

00:23:47.350 --> 00:23:50.700
I think that's sort of
being the unifying trend.

00:23:50.700 --> 00:23:54.962
And you think about it this way, anyone
that's my age or older, sort of under 30,

00:23:54.962 --> 00:23:59.649
the four main events that have defined our
lives History are 911 the Great Recession,

00:23:59.649 --> 00:24:03.405
the 2016 election,
the 2020 elections/the pandemic.

00:24:03.405 --> 00:24:07.128
Those are four big
failures of institutions.

00:24:07.128 --> 00:24:09.988
And so
that is what I've noticed sparked and

00:24:09.988 --> 00:24:14.630
thought about how young people
are really approaching this problem and

00:24:14.630 --> 00:24:19.053
less based on a, the older folks
are have screwed this up or that up.

00:24:19.053 --> 00:24:22.369
Because I certainly know at least from my
angle, that this seems to be the case that

00:24:22.369 --> 00:24:25.403
every younger generation says about
the older generation it's like hey,

00:24:25.403 --> 00:24:26.478
that older generations.

00:24:26.478 --> 00:24:29.324
Or is that older generations or so
then it seems like everyone screws up.

00:24:29.324 --> 00:24:32.485
So who really is the culprit?

00:24:32.485 --> 00:24:33.250
&gt;&gt; And all the old,

00:24:33.250 --> 00:24:36.664
older generations are always talking
about those slacker young people.

00:24:36.664 --> 00:24:41.684
I mean, they certainly were talking
about that when when I was 30s.

00:24:41.684 --> 00:24:46.769
You were out there protesting the Vietnam
War and stuff and they're like, yeah.

00:24:46.769 --> 00:24:49.039
[LAUGH] These kids,
they don't know anything.

00:24:49.039 --> 00:24:52.080
And yeah,
I think there is some something to that.

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:57.102
There are these stereotypes that
I think are more pervasive than

00:24:57.102 --> 00:25:02.218
actual experience with people of
different ages in the workforce

00:25:02.218 --> 00:25:06.790
that is the stereotypes
are stronger than the behaviors.

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:11.277
Lots of young people work with older
workers and older workers with younger and

00:25:11.277 --> 00:25:13.317
they don't think of them as older or

00:25:13.317 --> 00:25:15.782
younger they think of them as Mary and
Joe.

00:25:15.782 --> 00:25:19.702
And then you get to that and
I think that's again a way

00:25:19.702 --> 00:25:24.621
that work can bring people together
as opposed to separate them.

00:25:24.621 --> 00:25:29.091
But Jennifer, I think there's
really something true to this.

00:25:29.091 --> 00:25:32.209
Old Joe is sitting in that CEO spot and

00:25:32.209 --> 00:25:36.544
then as long if he's going to
work to lady what do I do?

00:25:36.544 --> 00:25:39.355
What happens to the bench?

00:25:39.355 --> 00:25:44.172
And what happens to young people
when older workers stay in jobs for

00:25:44.172 --> 00:25:45.816
a long period of time?

00:25:45.816 --> 00:25:51.489
What I believe is happening there is
that's real in some companies and

00:25:51.489 --> 00:25:55.250
but it is not true for
the macro level economy.

00:25:55.250 --> 00:26:00.382
At the macro level, as I was saying
earlier, the more older people in

00:26:00.382 --> 00:26:06.595
the workforce, the more money there is to
spend and the more jobs that get created.

00:26:06.595 --> 00:26:09.618
So one level,
there's no evidence for that, but

00:26:09.618 --> 00:26:13.589
not if you work in that company
where all Joe is sitting at the top.

00:26:13.589 --> 00:26:18.284
And so there is something real,
to that and younger worker,

00:26:18.284 --> 00:26:23.546
younger people today are just facing
a really hard time in history.

00:26:23.546 --> 00:26:25.576
They're not getting ahead.

00:26:25.576 --> 00:26:30.282
The rates that their parents did,
they're not able to buy homes as young as

00:26:30.282 --> 00:26:34.782
their parents did, or pay off debt
at the same rates or start families.

00:26:34.782 --> 00:26:39.685
And we're seeing all of that kind
of get reflected through lots of

00:26:39.685 --> 00:26:44.235
different milestones and
younger people are reaching those

00:26:44.235 --> 00:26:48.805
milestones at older ages than
their prior generations did.

00:26:48.805 --> 00:26:53.971
This is a problem I would resist
blaming it on older generations like

00:26:53.971 --> 00:26:59.607
you did this cause I don't think any,
I'm not aware of having done that.

00:26:59.607 --> 00:27:04.552
It's so, but it's happened and
I think all of us need to look at that and

00:27:04.552 --> 00:27:09.022
figure out a way that all of us
can help younger people do better.

00:27:09.022 --> 00:27:10.613
&gt;&gt; So what do you attribute it to?

00:27:10.613 --> 00:27:13.677
So I mean, I think there's no, I mean,
the evidence is just clear on this, right?

00:27:13.677 --> 00:27:15.843
The younger people are reaching
those milestones that later use.

00:27:15.843 --> 00:27:19.205
So that's just object seems
to be objectively true.

00:27:19.205 --> 00:27:21.384
What do you attribute that to?

00:27:21.384 --> 00:27:26.563
I'm curious to any anyone can weigh in
here, because you what, I understand

00:27:26.563 --> 00:27:31.662
you don't want to tribute it to
the behavior of older people, but I don't,

00:27:31.662 --> 00:27:35.636
I'm curious what the attribution is
&gt;&gt; The research looks

00:27:35.636 --> 00:27:37.586
like it's about money.

00:27:37.586 --> 00:27:42.224
When you graduate from school and
you've got $100,000 in debt,

00:27:42.224 --> 00:27:43.806
you don't get married.

00:27:43.806 --> 00:27:44.736
You don't have kids.

00:27:44.736 --> 00:27:45.571
You don't buy a house.

00:27:45.571 --> 00:27:47.379
You don't start a retirement account.

00:27:47.379 --> 00:27:51.414
Because you got to pay off the debt, and
that's what's happening that looks like

00:27:51.414 --> 00:27:55.298
the financial analysis that my colleagues
have done at the longevity center.

00:27:55.298 --> 00:27:57.151
It looks like that's what's accounting for
this.

00:27:57.151 --> 00:28:03.324
So at the root of it, it's having
the resources to be able to move forward.

00:28:03.324 --> 00:28:06.086
&gt;&gt; [CROSSTALK]
&gt;&gt; We'll only add one more thing to this

00:28:06.086 --> 00:28:09.211
though, this has been going on for
generations.

00:28:09.211 --> 00:28:14.078
Jello Streeter and
some of her colleagues again at the center

00:28:14.078 --> 00:28:18.191
did a study where they
compare different cohorts.

00:28:18.191 --> 00:28:25.752
And what they said the ideal age was to
get married to have kids to buy a house.

00:28:25.752 --> 00:28:32.275
And then she asked people of different
cohorts and when did you get to that age?

00:28:32.275 --> 00:28:33.771
And for generations,

00:28:33.771 --> 00:28:38.662
the age at which people achieve these
milestones has been getting older.

00:28:38.662 --> 00:28:43.077
So this isn't something that
suddenly appeared in today's youth.

00:28:43.077 --> 00:28:47.175
It's been getting older and
older gradually for decades.

00:28:47.175 --> 00:28:51.492
The sad part is that
the ideal age at which these

00:28:51.492 --> 00:28:56.045
milestones are achieved
hasn't changed a bit.

00:28:56.045 --> 00:29:01.492
So young people today are saying I should
have own a home by the time I'm 30.

00:29:01.492 --> 00:29:02.700
Most people aren't.

00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:07.502
But the idea that's the perception and
I think that's what also contributes to

00:29:07.502 --> 00:29:11.960
people feeling like they're being
left behind or they're not on track.

00:29:11.960 --> 00:29:13.731
There's something wrong
with them individually.

00:29:13.731 --> 00:29:18.549
But in fact, these trends have been
going on for a long, long time.

00:29:18.549 --> 00:29:23.189
&gt;&gt; Coming in some people that should
be that to a divestment in support of

00:29:23.189 --> 00:29:26.826
human capital in particular education,
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:29:26.826 --> 00:29:30.172
&gt;&gt; And so I wonder how it like this
takes us kind of to politics, right,

00:29:30.172 --> 00:29:32.425
because that's basically that's policy.

00:29:32.425 --> 00:29:37.456
And you could argue that the people
in power politically are aging and

00:29:37.456 --> 00:29:40.502
that might be somewhat zero sum, right?

00:29:40.502 --> 00:29:45.361
And unlike the workforce, so
I wonder how you think about that aging

00:29:45.361 --> 00:29:50.222
in politics should we try to do
something about the way our political

00:29:50.222 --> 00:29:54.251
system works in terms of term limits or
something else.

00:29:54.251 --> 00:29:59.002
To make sure that diversity we see say
example, for example in the workplace is

00:29:59.002 --> 00:30:03.691
also reflected among the people who
are making decisions in terms of policy.

00:30:03.691 --> 00:30:06.310
&gt;&gt; Before I sorry go ahead, Laura.

00:30:06.310 --> 00:30:08.434
&gt;&gt; No, I was just putting thumbs up.

00:30:08.434 --> 00:30:11.172
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yeah, no before it before I answer that

00:30:11.172 --> 00:30:16.077
question directly, the one issue that
I've noticed that really affects

00:30:16.077 --> 00:30:21.218
politics right now and you see this across
sort of the last couple of decades,

00:30:21.218 --> 00:30:24.809
is not just an age issue,
but actually a brain drain.

00:30:24.809 --> 00:30:29.091
I mean writ large, you see your smartest
people not going into public service.

00:30:29.091 --> 00:30:31.147
They're not going to social courses.

00:30:31.147 --> 00:30:36.127
They're not going into basic public
institutions that actually affect every

00:30:36.127 --> 00:30:37.796
piece of your daily life.

00:30:37.796 --> 00:30:41.346
You want your smartest McKenzie slash
whatever the case might be, and

00:30:41.346 --> 00:30:43.039
I can say that because I'm at the GSP.

00:30:43.039 --> 00:30:43.765
If we were in Kansas-
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:30:43.765 --> 00:30:45.230
&gt;&gt; I would not be saying the McKenzie

00:30:45.230 --> 00:30:46.968
consultant, but here we can say that.

00:30:46.968 --> 00:30:51.312
You want them going to be your mayor,
to run your local city council.

00:30:51.312 --> 00:30:54.140
You need people to be using basic
business skills that you learned and

00:30:54.140 --> 00:30:55.225
you're not seeing that.

00:30:55.225 --> 00:30:57.253
And I think, and this is the connection.

00:30:57.253 --> 00:31:00.547
The reason why that exists is
because you see your role models and

00:31:00.547 --> 00:31:01.778
politics being so old.

00:31:01.778 --> 00:31:06.448
I think there's one this stigma that you
can only enter politics once your hair

00:31:06.448 --> 00:31:07.523
starts growing.

00:31:07.523 --> 00:31:10.834
And I mean, that's only reinforced by
the fact that the current, for example,

00:31:10.834 --> 00:31:14.111
one of the Senators on the Republican
side just agreed to run for re-election.

00:31:14.111 --> 00:31:16.464
He's 88 isn't Chuck Grassley?

00:31:16.464 --> 00:31:20.089
I mean,
nothing against Senator Grassley with it.

00:31:20.089 --> 00:31:23.107
That's a really old age and
you can cast your own judgment on that.

00:31:23.107 --> 00:31:25.506
But that I mean the fact that
those are your role models and

00:31:25.506 --> 00:31:27.657
that's the age there's
just an issue there with,

00:31:27.657 --> 00:31:30.018
you're not going to have your
younger people going in.

00:31:30.018 --> 00:31:34.846
The second I think issue with regards to
having older politicians in power is that

00:31:34.846 --> 00:31:39.108
naturally change happens and
you want your leaders to be associated and

00:31:39.108 --> 00:31:41.894
understand that change very strategically.

00:31:41.894 --> 00:31:45.493
A core example of this is the hearings
around social media companies.

00:31:45.493 --> 00:31:49.924
I mean, the amount of fun that Senators
and members of Congress got made of,

00:31:49.924 --> 00:31:52.763
because they didn't know
what Facebook was is,

00:31:52.763 --> 00:31:56.659
you can't be governing when you
don't understand the policies.

00:31:56.659 --> 00:31:59.392
And so we can fix that,
we can educate, engage, but again,

00:31:59.392 --> 00:32:02.394
I think there's a broader issue and
it gets to your question.

00:32:02.394 --> 00:32:04.938
Dr. Lowery that do you want term limits?

00:32:04.938 --> 00:32:08.279
Do you want some sort of institutional
check, I think across the board.

00:32:08.279 --> 00:32:10.879
And again, this goes to the point
of anti institutionalism.

00:32:10.879 --> 00:32:11.618
Anti elitism.

00:32:11.618 --> 00:32:15.331
There's a strain and an anxiety against
people that have been empowered for

00:32:15.331 --> 00:32:16.326
a really long time.

00:32:16.326 --> 00:32:18.133
You're really old CEO, for example,

00:32:18.133 --> 00:32:20.661
is I think term limits
are supported across the board.

00:32:20.661 --> 00:32:24.973
And not just by younger people,
but also by older politicians.

00:32:24.973 --> 00:32:28.425
One of the big fears that a lot of
political leaders have actually by folks

00:32:28.425 --> 00:32:31.594
like, for example, Nancy Pelosi
recently said in an interview,

00:32:31.594 --> 00:32:34.837
the reason why she's still in office
is not because she wants to be.

00:32:34.837 --> 00:32:37.325
But because she's scared that no one
else is there to take the mantle.

00:32:37.325 --> 00:32:40.254
So they're not going to say necessarily
that they're there because they

00:32:40.254 --> 00:32:40.948
want to hang out.

00:32:40.948 --> 00:32:44.506
I mean, I totally understand that
a lot of them want to go off and

00:32:44.506 --> 00:32:46.500
do their things beyond politics.

00:32:46.500 --> 00:32:47.504
But there's a nervousness and

00:32:47.504 --> 00:32:49.636
anxiety that people aren't there
to ready to take up the mantle.

00:32:49.636 --> 00:32:53.313
So you have this vicious cycle where
new talent isn't coming in older talent

00:32:53.313 --> 00:32:54.640
distinct because of that.

00:32:54.640 --> 00:32:58.574
And as a result,
you only have your oldest town at the top.

00:32:58.574 --> 00:32:59.459
&gt;&gt; Do not be afraid.

00:32:59.459 --> 00:33:02.710
And then you just tell me the young
people are anti institution.

00:33:02.710 --> 00:33:06.458
Why would I want to pursue the government?

00:33:06.458 --> 00:33:08.503
&gt;&gt; Well see there's a difference there.

00:33:08.503 --> 00:33:11.917
It's not that they're anti institution in
the sense that they hate the government.

00:33:11.917 --> 00:33:16.487
It's that there's a mistrust in
the current leadership of various

00:33:16.487 --> 00:33:17.617
institutions.

00:33:17.617 --> 00:33:21.890
It's not that people hate people in suits,
right?

00:33:21.890 --> 00:33:26.032
No one, that the idea you have to control
the institutions to create change.

00:33:26.032 --> 00:33:28.149
It's that there's a mistrust and

00:33:28.149 --> 00:33:33.051
distrust in how those institutions have
been operating, which is different.

00:33:33.051 --> 00:33:36.301
Because no one's trying to burn down
institutions people are just trying to

00:33:36.301 --> 00:33:38.393
reform them,which is
a critical distinction.

00:33:38.393 --> 00:33:40.773
&gt;&gt; This is where incubators and

00:33:40.773 --> 00:33:45.335
organizations that can help
identify people to run for

00:33:45.335 --> 00:33:49.909
elected office and
support them and help fundraiser.

00:33:49.909 --> 00:33:55.005
And have those resources draw from I mean,
making the decision to run for office.

00:33:55.005 --> 00:34:00.521
It requires having a job that gives
you that flexibility to campaign,

00:34:00.521 --> 00:34:04.460
and if you win to take on
those responsibilities.

00:34:04.460 --> 00:34:10.549
And maintain a paid job with a wage for
your lifestyle.

00:34:10.549 --> 00:34:16.606
For a lot of public servant jobs
the pay is just not there for

00:34:16.606 --> 00:34:23.790
it to be this aspirational career
you don't go into it for the money.

00:34:23.790 --> 00:34:29.129
But it all comes back to money.

00:34:29.129 --> 00:34:34.461
If people don't have a lot of
the financial resources to be able

00:34:34.461 --> 00:34:40.812
to support themselves as they continue
on this additional goal of theirs.

00:34:40.812 --> 00:34:44.541
They're not going to feel that there
is space for them to give it a shot.

00:34:47.314 --> 00:34:52.578
&gt;&gt; I mean for perspective, the salary
of a senate page or Senate clerk right

00:34:52.578 --> 00:34:58.116
who's just working on the hill
first year out, is I think $32,000.

00:34:58.116 --> 00:35:01.921
No bonuses and
the most basic insurance benefits.

00:35:01.921 --> 00:35:03.905
They're very smart, intelligent people.

00:35:03.905 --> 00:35:08.105
But why would you in your right
mind choose to go live in DC and

00:35:08.105 --> 00:35:11.804
be paid $32,000 it's
just not going to cut it.

00:35:11.804 --> 00:35:15.639
And so I think Jennifer is absolutely
right that you have to think about pay.

00:35:15.639 --> 00:35:19.388
There's a little bit of a fear
of opening up incubation

00:35:19.388 --> 00:35:23.555
competitions on political things and
the fear around that.

00:35:23.555 --> 00:35:28.748
Is whether or not it just depends on
who's running those competitions.

00:35:28.748 --> 00:35:31.427
And what are the standards and
what are the rules.

00:35:31.427 --> 00:35:35.674
But that's not a conversation you
heard there, but on the same page.

00:35:37.485 --> 00:35:42.640
&gt;&gt; We live in a gerontocracy,
and it is a problem.

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:49.372
We are being governed by
a very old white male.

00:35:49.372 --> 00:35:54.178
Elite group of people, and
although there have been some changes in

00:35:54.178 --> 00:35:57.566
that there haven't been
enough changes in it.

00:35:57.566 --> 00:36:00.597
What I would add to the need for

00:36:00.597 --> 00:36:05.696
younger people representing
populations in DC.

00:36:05.696 --> 00:36:08.819
Would be some kind of policies,

00:36:08.819 --> 00:36:13.504
that would review any
kind of new legislation to

00:36:13.504 --> 00:36:18.919
evaluate how it will affect
different generations.

00:36:18.919 --> 00:36:23.428
A colleague of mine at Harvard, Lisa
Beckman has been advocating for this so

00:36:23.428 --> 00:36:25.971
that we would have some
kind of a committee.

00:36:25.971 --> 00:36:29.863
Let's say you're going to increase
payments in social security.

00:36:29.863 --> 00:36:33.072
And what you would have to do before
you did that you could do it and

00:36:33.072 --> 00:36:34.806
you could advocate for whatever.

00:36:34.806 --> 00:36:37.505
But you have to say,
this is what it's going to do for

00:36:37.505 --> 00:36:41.764
people who are 10 years younger,
20 years younger, 30 years younger.

00:36:41.764 --> 00:36:43.503
And so that we'd be able to see that.

00:36:43.503 --> 00:36:46.617
You can imagine the same
kind of things for climate and

00:36:46.617 --> 00:36:50.317
all sort of different kind of
policies that could be advocated.

00:36:50.317 --> 00:36:54.370
I think we have reached this point
with an age-diverse society.

00:36:54.370 --> 00:37:00.663
Where we need to think deeply about
how the changes that we advocate for

00:37:00.663 --> 00:37:07.395
are going to affect not just the current
generation but future generations.

00:37:07.395 --> 00:37:10.215
&gt;&gt; So one more question for each of you.

00:37:10.215 --> 00:37:13.044
What do you think is
the most pressing issue?

00:37:13.044 --> 00:37:18.105
What would you want to leave people
thinking about with respect to age and

00:37:18.105 --> 00:37:22.840
re imagining work, what we need to
do to have a vibrant workforce?

00:37:22.840 --> 00:37:30.785
And society going forward the most
pressing issue nearby Laura Christensen.

00:37:30.785 --> 00:37:31.710
&gt;&gt; Sure.

00:37:31.710 --> 00:37:34.605
It's a lack of imagination.

00:37:34.605 --> 00:37:38.140
We need to ask the question,
what are the opportunities here and

00:37:38.140 --> 00:37:41.347
instead we have this white
knuckle approach of bracing for

00:37:41.347 --> 00:37:45.290
the fall and my gosh, we're going to
get the old people and workforce.

00:37:45.290 --> 00:37:47.424
And what's going to happen to the young
people and we're just getting.

00:37:47.424 --> 00:37:51.545
And instead we need to just kind of
take a step back for a minute and say,

00:37:51.545 --> 00:37:55.613
here's the workforce today because
people are going to work longer.

00:37:55.613 --> 00:37:58.374
And we're going to have old
people we know that now.

00:37:58.374 --> 00:38:02.225
And to start to say,
how do we exploit that?

00:38:02.225 --> 00:38:06.555
[LAUGH].
Optimally in ways that improve life for

00:38:06.555 --> 00:38:07.926
everybody.

00:38:07.926 --> 00:38:09.623
That's the question and

00:38:09.623 --> 00:38:13.660
I think we spend a lot of time
worrying about the problems and

00:38:13.660 --> 00:38:19.345
what we really should be doing is thinking
about solutions and new opportunities.

00:38:19.345 --> 00:38:22.447
&gt;&gt; Great, Jennifer, what do you think?

00:38:22.447 --> 00:38:28.424
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think related, I mean.

00:38:28.424 --> 00:38:32.194
The way that so many people have been
working in the last 18 months is

00:38:32.194 --> 00:38:35.327
completely different from
what they have known before.

00:38:35.327 --> 00:38:39.793
It has presented such opportunity
in the way of flexible work.

00:38:39.793 --> 00:38:43.671
That I think a main challenge up to,

00:38:43.671 --> 00:38:51.561
the people in charge is understanding
how workplaces can be flexible and

00:38:51.561 --> 00:38:58.826
accommodate the needs of their
employees at various life stages.

00:38:58.826 --> 00:39:03.755
Right now I'm doing some reporting
on women being forced out

00:39:03.755 --> 00:39:07.439
of the labor force due
to ongoing childcare.

00:39:07.439 --> 00:39:10.502
And dependent care needs.

00:39:10.502 --> 00:39:15.142
But employers have shown that
they have the resources if

00:39:15.142 --> 00:39:17.516
they want to invest in women.

00:39:17.516 --> 00:39:21.848
And invest in underrepresented employees,

00:39:21.848 --> 00:39:28.235
they have the levers to pull on and
that varies so much throughout.

00:39:28.235 --> 00:39:33.951
Life stages into the aging workforce and
I think there's so

00:39:33.951 --> 00:39:39.220
much opportunity there we
can really be pushing more.

00:39:39.220 --> 00:39:41.123
&gt;&gt; Mani, what do you think?

00:39:41.123 --> 00:39:44.818
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I think, I'll just give
an example of our organization.

00:39:44.818 --> 00:39:47.518
So, we actually faced the totally opposite
problem which is that we just have too

00:39:47.518 --> 00:39:48.202
many young people.

00:39:48.202 --> 00:39:50.745
And and the entire dynamic
is focused on young people.

00:39:50.745 --> 00:39:53.430
Now that's for good reason because
we're student led movement and

00:39:53.430 --> 00:39:56.553
we're trying to improve the way that
young people are having conversations.

00:39:56.553 --> 00:39:58.173
So you want young people to help them.

00:39:58.173 --> 00:40:01.405
But actually the problem that we're
running into now is that there's both

00:40:01.405 --> 00:40:02.390
a lack of experience.

00:40:02.390 --> 00:40:06.490
And also, we want smarter people than us
in our leadership teams that we can be

00:40:06.490 --> 00:40:07.953
making better decisions.

00:40:07.953 --> 00:40:10.189
And so this goes to Laura's point,
which is.

00:40:10.189 --> 00:40:11.163
That's our situation.

00:40:11.163 --> 00:40:11.872
So how do we fix it?

00:40:11.872 --> 00:40:16.100
It wasn't thinking about older young
people it was who can fill this skill gap

00:40:16.100 --> 00:40:17.737
which is we need experience.

00:40:17.737 --> 00:40:20.832
We need smarter folks and we people
that have made mistakes in the past so

00:40:20.832 --> 00:40:21.772
they can get better.

00:40:21.772 --> 00:40:24.257
And they can teach us so
we have to make those mistakes.

00:40:24.257 --> 00:40:26.470
Who fits the bill people
lots of experience?

00:40:26.470 --> 00:40:28.216
Got lots of experience older people.

00:40:28.216 --> 00:40:32.419
And so I think rather than looking at the
situation as sort of Laura's describing

00:40:32.419 --> 00:40:36.936
Jennifer there was just saying in relation
to the workplace is structured is it's not

00:40:36.936 --> 00:40:38.271
necessarily a problem.

00:40:38.271 --> 00:40:39.237
It's just a situation.

00:40:39.237 --> 00:40:43.250
The situation is that we've got huge age
gaps and you got a lot of old people,

00:40:43.250 --> 00:40:44.454
a lot of young people.

00:40:44.454 --> 00:40:46.897
It's a situation is not a problem yet.

00:40:46.897 --> 00:40:50.430
The problem becomes when we look at it
as something that's insurmountable but

00:40:50.430 --> 00:40:52.309
actually it's filling a huge value gap.

00:40:52.309 --> 00:40:55.756
I mean right now your younger folks
are bringing a very diverse and

00:40:55.756 --> 00:40:57.270
interesting perspective.

00:40:57.270 --> 00:40:58.143
They've gotten you skills.

00:40:58.143 --> 00:41:00.211
They've got new technologies and

00:41:00.211 --> 00:41:03.851
you can harness the older wisdom
to not be obstacles to that.

00:41:03.851 --> 00:41:08.374
But to be jet fuel to propel that
forward without making the mistakes that

00:41:08.374 --> 00:41:10.088
they've made in the past.

00:41:10.088 --> 00:41:13.217
&gt;&gt; Great Well, all of you are very
optimistic about the future.

00:41:13.217 --> 00:41:14.519
I really appreciate that.

00:41:14.519 --> 00:41:15.159
[LAUGH].

00:41:15.159 --> 00:41:19.514
And hope that the way things develop,
support that optimism.

00:41:19.514 --> 00:41:22.493
But I really appreciate
the time that you brought so

00:41:22.493 --> 00:41:24.368
I can talk with you all tonight.

00:41:24.368 --> 00:41:26.820
So thanks so much for taking the time.

00:41:26.820 --> 00:41:27.679
&gt;&gt; Thanks Fred.

00:41:27.679 --> 00:41:28.944
&gt;&gt; Thank you for moderating.

00:41:28.944 --> 00:41:30.345
&gt;&gt; Like wise thank you.

00:41:30.345 --> 00:41:31.357
&gt;&gt; Thanks.

00:41:31.357 --> 00:41:35.558
So next week I'll be joined by
Rachel Carlson of Gilt Education.

00:41:35.558 --> 00:41:40.773
And David Osborne, the author reinventing
American schools to discuss education.

00:41:40.773 --> 00:41:42.522
So I hope that you all join that.

00:41:42.522 --> 00:41:43.128
Thanks so much.

00:41:43.128 --> 00:41:43.865
Have a good night.

